# A controlled Pandoc document bridge

This chapter is authoritative for `src/publication-pandoc.ts`. It prepares an
in-memory Pandoc document from `parseProject` without running Pandoc, tangling
source, loading a template, or writing files. The caller supplies a trusted
contribution renderer; ordinary author content never becomes raw output.

Preparation has two phases. First validate the graph and every document's full
Markdown token stream, retaining contribution placeholders inside their actual
containers. Only after the entire project passes validation may callbacks replace
those placeholders. A late unsupported image or missing reference therefore
cannot cause an earlier contribution renderer to run.


## The supported JSON contract

Engine and Markdown-it imports are types. Annotation bodies use the shared
annotation parser; this bridge imports no Node filesystem/process facilities.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc dependencies>>=
import type Token = require('markdown-it/lib/token');
import { parseAnnotationBody, resolveTermHref, PublicationAnnotations } from './publication-annotations';
import type {
    GenerationDiagnostic, ParsedDocument, ParsedProject,
    PublicationContribution, PublicationLocation
} from './engine';
```

Pandoc 3.7.0.2 was checked with its JSON reader and writer. It emits API version
`[1,23,1,1]` and accepts the compatible `[1,23,1]` used here, normalizing that
version on round-trip. Attributes are the actual identifier/classes/key-value
triple, not HTML attribute objects. Metadata is deliberately empty in this slice.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc public types>>=
export type PandocAttr = [string, string[], [string, string][]];
export interface PandocDocument {
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/naming-convention -- Pandoc's wire-format key.
    'pandoc-api-version': [1, 23, 1];
    meta: Record<string, never>;
    blocks: PandocBlock[];
}
export interface PandocPreparationResult {
    document?: PandocDocument;
    diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[];
}
export type PandocInline =
    | { t: 'RawInline'; c: ['latex', string] }
    | { t: 'Str'; c: string }
    | { t: 'Space' | 'SoftBreak' | 'LineBreak' }
    | { t: 'Code'; c: [PandocAttr, string] }
    | { t: 'Emph' | 'Strong' | 'Strikeout'; c: PandocInline[] }
    | { t: 'Link'; c: [PandocAttr, PandocInline[], [string, string]] };
```

`RawBlock` and `RawInline` are reserved for trusted renderers. The Markdown walker
never constructs either; semantic annotation Divs and Links remain data until the
LaTeX adapter materializes them. Tables, images, raw tokens,
and other extensions require an explicit future implementation, not a fallback
that silently loses content. Ordered-list style and delimiter are tagged Pandoc
enumerations; Markdown-it supplies the starting number and marker.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc public types>>=+
export type PandocListAttributes = [number, { t: 'Decimal' }, { t: 'Period' | 'OneParen' }];
export type PandocLeafBlock =
    | { t: 'Plain' | 'Para'; c: PandocInline[] }
    | { t: 'Header'; c: [number, PandocAttr, PandocInline[]] }
    | { t: 'HorizontalRule' }
    | { t: 'CodeBlock'; c: [PandocAttr, string] }
    | { t: 'RawBlock'; c: ['latex', string] };
export type PandocBlock = PandocLeafBlock
    | { t: 'BlockQuote'; c: PandocBlock[] }
    | { t: 'BulletList'; c: PandocBlock[][] }
    | { t: 'OrderedList'; c: [PandocListAttributes, PandocBlock[][]] }
    | { t: 'Div'; c: [PandocAttr, PandocBlock[]] };
```

Pending blocks differ only at contribution slots and containers holding them.
They never cross the public API boundary. This small explicit representation
keeps validation separate from callbacks without a generic visitor framework or
string placeholders that could collide with author text.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc pending types>>=
type PendingBlock = PandocLeafBlock
    | { t: 'Contribution'; part: PublicationContribution }
    | { t: 'Asset'; asset: PublicationAsset }
    | { t: 'BlockQuote'; c: PendingBlock[] }
    | { t: 'BulletList'; c: PendingBlock[][] }
    | { t: 'OrderedList'; c: [PandocListAttributes, PendingBlock[][]] }
    | { t: 'Div'; c: [PandocAttr, PendingBlock[]] };
export interface PublicationAsset {
    kind: 'image' | 'mermaid'; source: string; caption: string; at: PublicationLocation;
}
export type AssetRenderer = (asset: PublicationAsset) => PandocBlock[];
type ContributionRenderer = (part: PublicationContribution) => PandocBlock[];
const maximumNesting = 128;
function emptyAttributes(): PandocAttr { return ['', [], []]; }
```

## Diagnostics and graph validation

Positions remain zero-based. Markdown-it does not provide precise positions for
inline children, so their diagnostics use the containing inline block's starting
line and column zero. Semantic reference diagnostics retain the engine's exact
source locations. No fabricated character offsets are inferred from rendered text.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc diagnostics>>=
function report(
    diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[], at: PublicationLocation, code: string, message: string
): void {
    diagnostics.push({ ...at, code, message });
}
function tokenLocation(document: ParsedDocument, token?: Token, fallback = 0): PublicationLocation {
    return { path: document.path, line: token?.map?.[0] ?? fallback, column: 0 };
}
function unsupported(diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[], at: PublicationLocation, type: string): void {
    const raw = /^(html|raw|tex|latex)(_|$)/.test(type);
    report(diagnostics, at, raw ? 'unsupported-raw-content' : 'unsupported-pandoc-token',
        raw ? `Raw author content is unsupported: ${type}` : `Unsupported Markdown token: ${type}`);
}
```

Existing parser diagnostics are a hard gate. For otherwise accepted projects,
check every reference, even in unused fragments, before inspecting dependency
cycles. Reference modifiers are invalid regardless of whether their target exists.
Names and boundaries come from publication segments: a literal `\<<name>>` is
never rescanned into a semantic reference here.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc graph validation>>=
interface Dependency { target: string; at: PublicationLocation; }
function validateReferences(project: ParsedProject, diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[]): void {
    const families = new Map(project.publication.fragments.map(family => [family.target, family]));
    const edges = new Map<string, Dependency[]>();
    for (const family of families.values()) { edges.set(family.target, []); }
    for (const part of project.publication.contributions) {
        for (const segment of part.segments) {
            if (segment.kind !== 'reference') { continue; }
            if (segment.modifier !== undefined) {
                report(diagnostics, segment.at, 'invalid-reference-modifier',
                    `Invalid definition operator on reference: ${segment.name}`);
            }
            if (segment.resolution.kind === 'missing' || !families.has(segment.resolution.target)) {
                report(diagnostics, segment.at, 'missing-fragment', `Missing fragment: ${segment.name}`);
            } else {
                edges.get(part.fragmentTarget)?.push({ target: segment.resolution.target, at: segment.at });
            }
        }
    }
    validateCycles(edges, families, diagnostics);
}
```

An iterative depth-first walk uses active and finished sets. It never expands a
fragment, so deep or exponentially expanding graphs require only graph-sized
storage and traversal. A back edge reports a cycle at the reference that closes
it; repeated back edges may produce separate source diagnostics.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc graph validation>>=+
function validateCycles(
    edges: Map<string, Dependency[]>, families: Map<string, { name: string }>,
    diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[]
): void {
    const active = new Set<string>(), finished = new Set<string>();
    for (const target of edges.keys()) {
        if (finished.has(target)) { continue; }
        const stack = [{ target, index: 0 }];
        active.add(target);
        while (stack.length) {
            const frame = stack[stack.length - 1];
            const edge = edges.get(frame.target)![frame.index++];
            if (!edge) {
                active.delete(frame.target); finished.add(frame.target); stack.pop();
            } else if (active.has(edge.target)) {
                report(diagnostics, edge.at, 'fragment-cycle',
                    `Fragment cycle through: ${families.get(edge.target)!.name}`);
            } else if (!finished.has(edge.target)) {
                active.add(edge.target); stack.push({ target: edge.target, index: 0 });
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Document destinations and links

Each canonical document path gets a separate `d-` namespace with fixed-width
UTF-16 hexadecimal encoding. This is injective and independent of titles, line
numbers, and chapter order. Each document, including an empty one, has a `Div`
destination. Headings use a separate `h-` namespace encoding both document path
and allocated slug, separated by a hyphen (which cannot occur in the encoding).
Neither author text nor a URL is interpolated into raw TeX.

Generated HTML currently uses plain Markdown-it headings without IDs. The
extension installs fence/document renderers on the host preview parser, leaving
heading IDs to VS Code; it supplies no slug implementation to reuse. Publication
therefore defines its own explicit policy rather than claiming exact preview
compatibility. Common lowercase, hyphenated links work where hosts use that
convention; duplicate and punctuation behavior can differ.

Slugs use parsed visible text and code spans, including emphasis and link labels,
not Markdown delimiters or link URLs. Normalize to NFC, lowercase without locale,
remove Unicode punctuation/symbols except hyphens and underscores, then collapse
whitespace to hyphens. Normalize the final slug to NFC again before grouping or
encoding: lowercasing and punctuation removal can create new composable sequences.
Empty results become `section`. Accents and non-Latin letters remain; fragment
matching is case-sensitive and NFC-normalized.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc heading identities>>=
interface DocumentDestination {
    id: string;
    headings: Map<string, string[]>;
    tokens: Map<Token, string>;
}
type PublicationTargets = Map<string, DocumentDestination>;
function headingSlug(inline?: Token): string {
    const text = (inline?.children ?? []).map(token => {
        if (token.type === 'text' || token.type === 'code_inline') { return token.content; }
        return token.type === 'softbreak' || token.type === 'hardbreak' ? ' ' : '';
    }).join('');
    return text.normalize('NFC').toLowerCase().replace(/[^\p{L}\p{M}\p{N}\s_-]/gu, '')
        .trim().replace(/\s+/gu, '-').normalize('NFC') || 'section';
}
```

Reserve every natural base slug before allocating duplicate suffixes. A unique
base keeps its slug; every member of a duplicate group gets `base-1`, `base-2`,
etc., skipping reserved or already allocated names. Thus literal `Foo-1` cannot
steal a duplicate `Foo` destination. An unnumbered duplicate link is ambiguous
and lists usable alternatives. Unlinked duplicates are valid.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc heading identities>>=+
function headingDestinations(document: ParsedDocument): DocumentDestination {
    const groups = new Map<string, Token[]>();
    document.tokens.forEach((token, index) => {
        if (token.type !== 'heading_open') { return; }
        const slug = headingSlug(document.tokens[index + 1]);
        const group = groups.get(slug) ?? [];
        group.push(token); groups.set(slug, group);
    });
    const destination: DocumentDestination = {
        id: documentTarget(document.path), headings: new Map(), tokens: new Map()
    };
    const reserved = new Set(groups.keys());
    for (const [base, tokens] of groups) {
        allocateHeadings(document.path, base, tokens, reserved, destination);
    }
    return destination;
}
```

The index scans the full stream before reading any links, so forward links,
setext headings and nested headings work without flattening their containers.
Identities do not depend on line offsets or chapter order. Unrelated headings do
not change IDs; renaming a heading/path or inserting a colliding slug can change
them. Duplicate ordinals follow source token order, not arbitrary edit-stable IDs.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc heading identities>>=+
function allocateHeadings(path: string, base: string, tokens: Token[], reserved: Set<string>,
    destination: DocumentDestination): void {
    const slugs: string[] = [];
    let ordinal = 1;
    for (const token of tokens) {
        let slug = base;
        if (tokens.length > 1) {
            do { slug = base + '-' + ordinal++; } while (reserved.has(slug));
        }
        reserved.add(slug); slugs.push(slug);
        const id = 'h-' + documentTarget(path).slice(2) + '-' + documentTarget(slug).slice(2);
        destination.tokens.set(token, id);
        destination.headings.set(slug, [id]);
    }
    if (tokens.length > 1) { destination.headings.set(base, slugs); }
}
```

```ts : <<publication Pandoc document links>>=
function documentTarget(path: string): string {
    let encoded = 'd-';
    for (let index = 0; index < path.length; index++) {
        encoded += path.charCodeAt(index).toString(16).padStart(4, '0');
    }
    return encoded;
}
function localDocumentPath(href: string, source: string): string | undefined {
    if (!href || /[?#\\\x00-\x20\x7f]/.test(href) || href.startsWith('/')) { return undefined; }
    let decoded: string;
    try { decoded = decodeURIComponent(href); } catch { return undefined; }
    if (/[?#\\\x00-\x1f\x7f]/.test(decoded) || decoded.startsWith('/')) { return undefined; }
    if (!/\.(html|literate)$/.test(decoded)) { return undefined; }
    const parts = source.split('/').slice(0, -1);
    for (const part of decoded.split('/')) {
        if (!part || part === '.') { continue; }
        if (part === '..') { if (!parts.length) { return undefined; } parts.pop(); }
        else { parts.push(part); }
    }
    return parts.join('/').replace(/\.html$/, '.literate');
}
```

Only HTTP, HTTPS, and mailto schemes are admitted as external links. Relative
links must resolve exactly to an included `.literate` document or its `.html`
alias. Percent-encoded path names and parent-directory navigation are supported
without permitting escape above the project root. Split the literal fragment
separator before decoding either component exactly once. Queries, empty fragments,
malformed escapes, control characters and unsafe paths remain unsupported.
Missing included documents, missing headings and ambiguous headings have distinct
diagnostics. Link text and titles remain Pandoc data, never TeX commands.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc document links>>=+
function safeExternalLink(href: string): boolean {
    try {
        if (/[\\\x00-\x20\x7f]/.test(href) || /[\x00-\x1f\x7f]/.test(decodeURIComponent(href))) { return false; }
        const url = new URL(href);
        if (/^https?:\/\//i.test(href)) { return !!url.hostname; }
        return /^mailto:/i.test(href) && url.protocol === 'mailto:' && !!url.pathname;
    } catch { return false; }
}
function linkDestination(
    href: string, document: ParsedDocument, targets: PublicationTargets,
    diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[], at: PublicationLocation
): string | undefined {
    if (/^[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*:/i.test(href)) {
        if (safeExternalLink(href)) { return href; }
    } else {
        const resolved = localLinkDestination(href, document, targets, diagnostics, at);
        if (resolved !== undefined) { return resolved || undefined; }
    }
    report(diagnostics, at, 'unsupported-publication-link',
        `Unsupported link (expected safe HTTP/HTTPS/mailto or an included document/heading without query): ${href}`);
    return undefined;
}
```

Local resolution returns an empty sentinel for an already diagnosed missing or
ambiguous destination, avoiding a second misleading unsupported-link diagnostic.
Whole-document links retain their original stable `Div` destinations.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc document links>>=+
function localLinkDestination(href: string, document: ParsedDocument, targets: PublicationTargets,
    diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[], at: PublicationLocation): string | undefined {
    const hash = href.indexOf('#');
    const path = hash === 0 ? document.path : localDocumentPath(hash < 0 ? href : href.slice(0, hash), document.path);
    let slug: string | undefined;
    if (hash >= 0) {
        try { slug = decodeURIComponent(href.slice(hash + 1)).normalize('NFC'); } catch { return undefined; }
        if (!slug || /[?#\\\x00-\x20\x7f]/.test(slug)) { return undefined; }
    }
    if (path === undefined) { return undefined; }
    const destination = targets.get(path);
    if (!destination) {
        report(diagnostics, at, 'missing-publication-document', `Document is not included: ${path}`);
        return '';
    }
    if (slug === undefined) { return '#' + destination.id; }
    return headingLinkDestination(destination, slug, path, diagnostics, at);
}
```

An ambiguous base maps to allocated slug choices, whereas a unique slug maps to
one opaque target. The map is private to this preparation and never mutates the
engine's tokens or attributes.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc document links>>=+
function headingLinkDestination(destination: DocumentDestination, slug: string, path: string,
    diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[], at: PublicationLocation): string {
    const matches = destination.headings.get(slug);
    if (!matches) {
        report(diagnostics, at, 'missing-publication-heading', `Missing heading: ${path}#${slug}`);
    } else if (matches.length > 1) {
        report(diagnostics, at, 'ambiguous-publication-heading',
            `Ambiguous heading: ${path}#${slug}; use ${matches.map(value => '#' + value).join(', ')}`);
    } else { return '#' + matches[0]; }
    return '';
}
```

## Reading inline tokens

The inline reader consumes matching pairs recursively. Ordinary text is split
into Pandoc words and spaces; HTML- or TeX-looking text is still ordinary text.
Code spans preserve their exact token content. Literal code is not scanned for
fragment references. The nesting limit turns excessively nested input into a
source diagnostic before JavaScript recursion becomes unsafe.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc inline reader>>=
interface InlineReader {
    annotations: PublicationAnnotations;
    document: ParsedDocument;
    tokens: readonly Token[];
    index: number;
    at: PublicationLocation;
    targets: PublicationTargets;
    diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[];
}
function textInlines(text: string): PandocInline[] {
    return text.split(/([ \t\r\n]+)/).filter(Boolean).map(value =>
        /^[ \t\r\n]+$/.test(value) ? { t: 'Space' } : { t: 'Str', c: value });
}
function readInlines(reader: InlineReader, stop?: string, depth = 0): PandocInline[] {
    const result: PandocInline[] = [];
    if (depth > maximumNesting) {
        report(reader.diagnostics, reader.at, 'publication-nesting-limit', 'Inline nesting exceeds 128 levels');
        reader.index = reader.tokens.length;
        return result;
    }
    <<publication Pandoc inline loop>>
    if (stop) { report(reader.diagnostics, reader.at, 'invalid-markdown-structure', `Missing ${stop}`); }
    return result;
}
```

Closers terminate only the matching container. Unsupported tokens, including
images and raw HTML tokens from a caller-provided parser extension, cannot be
silently interpreted as strings. Their diagnostics block the whole preparation.
Append text nodes iteratively: a flat, very long paragraph must not consume one
JavaScript argument per word and exceed the runtime's argument limit.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc inline loop>>=
while (reader.index < reader.tokens.length) {
    const token = reader.tokens[reader.index++];
    if (stop && token.type === stop) { return result; }
    switch (token.type) {
        case 'text':
                    for (const inline of textInlines(token.content)) { result.push(inline); }
                    break;
        case 'code_inline': result.push({ t: 'Code', c: [emptyAttributes(), token.content] }); break;
        case 'softbreak': result.push({ t: 'SoftBreak' }); break;
        case 'hardbreak': result.push({ t: 'LineBreak' }); break;
        case 'em_open': result.push({ t: 'Emph', c: readInlines(reader, 'em_close', depth + 1) }); break;
        case 'strong_open': result.push({ t: 'Strong', c: readInlines(reader, 'strong_close', depth + 1) }); break;
        case 's_open': result.push({ t: 'Strikeout', c: readInlines(reader, 's_close', depth + 1) }); break;
        case 'link_open': result.push(readLink(reader, token, depth)); break;
        default: unsupported(reader.diagnostics, reader.at, token.type);
    }
}
```

Link validation still visits the complete label before returning. The temporary
empty destination on failure is never exposed, because any diagnostic suppresses
the public document and all renderer callbacks.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc inline reader>>=+
function readLink(reader: InlineReader, token: Token, depth: number): PandocInline {
    const original = token.attrGet('href') ?? '';
    const term = original.startsWith('term:');
    const resolved = term ? resolveTermHref(reader.annotations, original) : undefined;
    if (term && !resolved) {
        report(reader.diagnostics, reader.at, 'annotation-missing', `Unresolved TERM link: ${original}`);
    }
    const href = term ? '#' + (resolved ?? '') : linkDestination(original, reader.document,
        reader.targets, reader.diagnostics, reader.at);
    const label = readInlines(reader, 'link_close', depth + 1);
    return { t: 'Link', c: [['', term ? ['lit-term-use'] : [], []], label,
        [href ?? '', token.attrGet('title') ?? '']] };
}
```

## Reading the complete block stream

Markdown ranges in `ParsedDocument.blocks` may end inside lists or blockquotes.
They are therefore used only to locate accepted contributions, never rendered
independently. The block reader walks the complete `tokens` array with one shared
cursor, preserving nesting and authored order across each contribution.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc block reader>>=
interface BlockReader {
    annotations: PublicationAnnotations;
    annotationBody?: boolean;
    document: ParsedDocument;
    index: number;
    contributions: Map<number, PublicationContribution>;
    assets: boolean;
    targets: PublicationTargets;
    diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[];
}
function contributionIndex(
    document: ParsedDocument, parts: Map<string, PublicationContribution>, diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[]
): Map<number, PublicationContribution> {
    const result = new Map<number, PublicationContribution>();
    for (const block of document.blocks) {
        if (block.kind !== 'contribution') { continue; }
        const part = parts.get(block.target), token = document.tokens[block.tokenIndex];
        if (!part || part.tokenIndex !== block.tokenIndex || part.definition.path !== document.path
            || token?.type !== 'fence' || result.has(block.tokenIndex)) {
            report(diagnostics, tokenLocation(document, token), 'invalid-publication-snapshot',
                `Invalid contribution binding: ${block.target}`);
        } else { result.set(block.tokenIndex, part); }
    }
    return result;
}
```

Every block container expects its own closing token. Malformed or excessively
nested snapshots produce diagnostics rather than partially published output.
Parser-generated snapshots normally satisfy these structural checks already.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc block reader>>=+
function readBlocks(reader: BlockReader, stop?: string, depth = 0): PendingBlock[] {
    const result: PendingBlock[] = [];
    const tokens = reader.document.tokens;
    if (depth > maximumNesting) {
        report(reader.diagnostics, tokenLocation(reader.document, tokens[reader.index]),
            'publication-nesting-limit', 'Block nesting exceeds 128 levels');
        reader.index = tokens.length;
        return result;
    }
    while (reader.index < tokens.length) {
        const index = reader.index++, token = tokens[index];
        if (stop && token.type === stop) { return result; }
        result.push(...readBlock(reader, token, index, depth));
    }
    if (stop) {
        report(reader.diagnostics, tokenLocation(reader.document, tokens[tokens.length - 1]),
            'invalid-markdown-structure', `Missing ${stop}`);
    }
    return result;
}
```

Paragraphs inside tight lists become `Plain`, matching Markdown-it's hidden
paragraph markers. Headings retain their explicit level; the future writer may
choose chapter-level LaTeX division without changing this document structure.
Blockquotes and lists recurse over the same cursor, not isolated token slices.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc block reader>>=+
function readBlock(reader: BlockReader, token: Token, index: number, depth: number): PendingBlock[] {
    switch (token.type) {
        case 'paragraph_open': return readParagraph(reader, token);
        case 'heading_open': return [readHeading(reader, token)];
        case 'hr': return [{ t: 'HorizontalRule' }];
        case 'blockquote_open': return [{ t: 'BlockQuote', c: readBlocks(reader, 'blockquote_close', depth + 1) }];
        case 'bullet_list_open': return [{ t: 'BulletList', c: readList(reader, 'bullet_list_close', depth + 1) }];
        case 'ordered_list_open': return [readOrderedList(reader, token, depth)];
        case 'fence': return readFence(reader, token, index);
        case 'code_block': return [{ t: 'CodeBlock', c: [emptyAttributes(), token.content] }];
        default: unsupported(reader.diagnostics, tokenLocation(reader.document, token), token.type); return [];
    }
}
```

A text block must contain the inline token followed by its matching closer. The
children are already parsed Markdown; feeding `token.content` to a second parser
would introduce a different interpretation of escaping and links.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc block reader>>=+
<<publication Pandoc image paragraph>>
function readTextBlock(reader: BlockReader, opening: Token): PandocInline[] {
    const token = reader.document.tokens[reader.index];
    const at = tokenLocation(reader.document, token, opening.map?.[0] ?? 0);
    const stop = opening.type === 'heading_open' ? 'heading_close' : 'paragraph_close';
    if (token?.type !== 'inline') {
        report(reader.diagnostics, at, 'invalid-markdown-structure', `Expected inline after ${opening.type}`);
        return [];
    }
    reader.index++;
    const result = readInlines({ document: reader.document, tokens: token.children ?? [],
        index: 0, at, targets: reader.targets, diagnostics: reader.diagnostics, annotations: reader.annotations });
    if (reader.document.tokens[reader.index]?.type === stop) { reader.index++; }
    else { report(reader.diagnostics, at, 'invalid-markdown-structure', `Expected ${stop}`); }
    return result;
}
function readHeading(reader: BlockReader, token: Token): PandocLeafBlock {
    const level = /^h[1-6]$/.test(token.tag) ? Number(token.tag.slice(1)) : 0;
    if (!level) {
        report(reader.diagnostics, tokenLocation(reader.document, token), 'invalid-markdown-structure', 'Invalid heading level');
    }
    if (reader.document.tokens[reader.index]?.children?.some(child =>
        child.type === 'link_open' && child.attrGet('href')?.startsWith('term:'))) {
        report(reader.diagnostics, tokenLocation(reader.document, token), 'annotation-heading',
            'TERM links in headings are unsupported: use a prose link below the heading to avoid moving rail markers');
    }
    const id = reader.targets.get(reader.document.path)!.tokens.get(token)!;
    return { t: 'Header', c: [level, [id, [], []], readTextBlock(reader, token)] };
}
```

Lists consume item boundaries explicitly. An item may hold any supported block,
including nested lists and contribution placeholders. Empty item arrays are
valid; unknown list children still receive diagnostics.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc block reader>>=+
function readList(reader: BlockReader, stop: string, depth: number): PendingBlock[][] {
    const result: PendingBlock[][] = [], tokens = reader.document.tokens;
    while (reader.index < tokens.length) {
        const token = tokens[reader.index++];
        if (token.type === stop) { return result; }
        if (token.type === 'list_item_open') { result.push(readBlocks(reader, 'list_item_close', depth)); }
        else { unsupported(reader.diagnostics, tokenLocation(reader.document, token), token.type); }
    }
    report(reader.diagnostics, tokenLocation(reader.document, tokens[tokens.length - 1]),
        'invalid-markdown-structure', `Missing ${stop}`);
    return result;
}
function readOrderedList(reader: BlockReader, token: Token, depth: number): PendingBlock {
    const start = Number(token.attrGet('start') ?? '1');
    if (!Number.isSafeInteger(start) || start < 0 || !['.', ')'].includes(token.markup)) {
        report(reader.diagnostics, tokenLocation(reader.document, token), 'invalid-markdown-structure', 'Invalid ordered-list marker');
    }
    const attributes: PandocListAttributes = [start, { t: 'Decimal' }, { t: token.markup === ')' ? 'OneParen' : 'Period' }];
    return { t: 'OrderedList', c: [attributes, readList(reader, 'ordered_list_close', depth + 1)] };
}
```

All `SETTINGS` fences are omitted, matching current generation behavior, without
activating new settings. Mermaid is explicitly unsupported, including a named
contribution whose own language is Mermaid. Other ordinary fences are code data;
a tag-looking string there is not a contribution or dependency.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc block reader>>=+
function readFence(reader: BlockReader, token: Token, index: number): PendingBlock[] {
    if (token.info.trim() === 'SETTINGS') { return []; }
    if (!reader.annotationBody && /^(TERM|NOTE)(?:\s|$)/.test(token.info.trim())) {
        return readAnnotation(reader, index);
    }
    const part = reader.contributions.get(index);
    const language = part?.language ?? token.info.trim().split(/\s+/)[0];
    if (language.toLowerCase() === 'mermaid') {
        if (!part && reader.assets && token.info.trim().toLowerCase() === 'mermaid') {
            return [{ t: 'Asset', asset: { kind: 'mermaid', source: token.content, caption: '',
                at: tokenLocation(reader.document, token) } }];
        }
        report(reader.diagnostics, tokenLocation(reader.document, token), 'unsupported-mermaid',
            'Mermaid requires an ordinary fence and a publication asset renderer');
        return [];
    }
    if (part) { return [{ t: 'Contribution', part }]; }
    return [{ t: 'CodeBlock', c: [['', language ? [language] : [], []], token.content] }];
}
```

## Controlled annotation bodies

Annotation bodies use the shared parser and the same block/link validator as prose.
Their semantic Divs contain no raw author output. Validation includes all bodies
before any contribution or asset callback, including asset-collection preflight.
The shared collector rejects images, block code, raw content and other extensions.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc block reader>>=+
function readAnnotation(reader: BlockReader, index: number): PendingBlock[] {
    const matches = (item: { at: PublicationLocation; tokenIndex: number }): boolean =>
        item.at.path === reader.document.path && item.tokenIndex === index;
    const term = reader.annotations.terms.find(matches);
    const note = reader.annotations.notes.find(matches);
    const item = term ?? note;
    if (!item) {
        report(reader.diagnostics, tokenLocation(reader.document, reader.document.tokens[index]),
            'annotation-binding', 'Annotation fence has no shared annotation binding');
        return [];
    }
    const tokens = parseAnnotationBody(item.body);
    for (const token of tokens) {
        if (token.map) { token.map = [item.at.line + 1 + token.map[0], item.at.line + 1 + token.map[1]]; }
    }
    const body = readBlocks({ ...reader, annotationBody: true, assets: false,
        document: { ...reader.document, tokens }, index: 0, contributions: new Map() });
    return [{ t: 'Div', c: [[item.target, [term ? 'lit-term' : 'lit-note'], []], body] }];
}
```

## Standalone local image requests

An image occupies its own paragraph, including inside lists and quotes. Inline
images remain unsupported instead of silently losing surrounding prose. Captions
use the title when present, otherwise concatenate decoded plain-text children so
entities and escaped punctuation print correctly; formatted alt text is an explicit error. The optional trusted asset callback runs only after full token
validation. Without it, the existing unsupported-image diagnostic is retained.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc image paragraph>>=
function readParagraph(reader: BlockReader, opening: Token): PendingBlock[] {
    const inline = reader.document.tokens[reader.index];
    const children = inline?.children ?? [];
    const image = children.length === 1 && children[0].type === 'image' ? children[0] : undefined;
    if (!image || !reader.assets) {
        return [{ t: opening.hidden ? 'Plain' : 'Para', c: readTextBlock(reader, opening) }];
    }
    const at = tokenLocation(reader.document, inline);
    const caption = image.children ?? [];
    if (caption.some(child => child.type !== 'text')) {
        report(reader.diagnostics, at, 'unsupported-image-caption', 'Image alt text must be plain text');
    }
    reader.index++;
    if (reader.document.tokens[reader.index]?.type === 'paragraph_close') { reader.index++; }
    else { report(reader.diagnostics, at, 'invalid-markdown-structure', 'Expected paragraph_close'); }
    return [{ t: 'Asset', asset: { kind: 'image', at, source: image.attrGet('src') ?? '',
        caption: image.attrGet('title') || caption.map(child => child.content).join('') } }];
}
```

## Materializing trusted contributions

Only validated pending trees reach this phase. Contributions are replaced in
place with zero or more callback blocks, preserving surrounding containers. The
callback owns semantic code rendering and may deliberately emit LaTeX `RawBlock`
values. Its output is trusted renderer code, not an author-supplied AST or a
custom-template security boundary. Neither the callback nor this bridge should
mutate the shared parsed snapshot.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc materialization>>=
function materialize(
    pending: PendingBlock[], render: ContributionRenderer, diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[], asset?: AssetRenderer
): PandocBlock[] {
    const result: PandocBlock[] = [];
    for (const block of pending) {
        if (diagnostics.length) { break; }
        switch (block.t) {
            case 'Asset':
                try { result.push(...asset!(block.asset)); }
                catch (error) { report(diagnostics, block.asset.at, 'asset-render-failed', String(error)); }
                break;
            case 'Contribution':
                for (const rendered of renderPart(block.part, render, diagnostics)) { result.push(rendered); }
                break;
            case 'BlockQuote': result.push({ t: 'BlockQuote', c: materialize(block.c, render, diagnostics, asset) }); break;
            case 'BulletList': result.push({ t: 'BulletList', c: block.c.map(item => materialize(item, render, diagnostics, asset)) }); break;
            case 'OrderedList': result.push({ t: 'OrderedList', c: [block.c[0],
                block.c[1].map(item => materialize(item, render, diagnostics, asset))] }); break;
            case 'Div': result.push({ t: 'Div', c: [block.c[0], materialize(block.c[1], render, diagnostics, asset)] }); break;
            default: result.push(block);
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```

A callback exception is reported at that contribution's declaration. No public
document is returned, and later callbacks are not invoked. Earlier callbacks
cannot be undone, so callers should keep rendering free of external side effects.
The array check catches a common untyped-caller mistake, but is not presented as
validation or sanitization of the trusted renderer's complete output tree.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc materialization>>=+
function renderPart(
    part: PublicationContribution, render: ContributionRenderer, diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[]
): PandocBlock[] {
    try {
        const blocks = render(part);
        if (!Array.isArray(blocks)) { throw new Error('Contribution renderer must return a block array'); }
        return blocks;
    } catch (error) {
        report(diagnostics, part.definition, 'contribution-render-failed',
            `Contribution renderer failed: ${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}`);
        return [];
    }
}
```

## Preparing the document

The public API preserves document order and returns either a complete document
with no diagnostics or diagnostics without a document. Existing parse diagnostics
are copied, not mutated. Graph validation never calls the contribution renderer;
token validation still runs across all documents when graph errors are found,
so callers receive both graph and content diagnostics in deterministic order.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc preparation>>=
export function preparePandoc(
    project: ParsedProject, renderContribution: (part: PublicationContribution) => PandocBlock[],
    renderAsset?: AssetRenderer
): PandocPreparationResult {
    const diagnostics = project.diagnostics.map(diagnostic => ({ ...diagnostic }));
    if (diagnostics.length) { return { diagnostics }; }
    diagnostics.push(...project.annotations.diagnostics.map(diagnostic => ({ ...diagnostic })));
    if (diagnostics.length) { return { diagnostics }; }
    validateReferences(project, diagnostics);
    const targets = new Map(project.documents.map(document => [document.path, headingDestinations(document)]));
    const parts = new Map(project.publication.contributions.map(part => [part.target, part]));
    const pending: PendingBlock[] = project.documents.map(document => {
        const contributions = contributionIndex(document, parts, diagnostics);
        const body = readBlocks({ document, index: 0, contributions, targets, diagnostics,
            annotations: project.annotations, assets: !!renderAsset });
        return { t: 'Div', c: [[targets.get(document.path)!.id, [], []], body] };
    });
    if (diagnostics.length) { return { diagnostics }; }
    const blocks = materialize(pending, renderContribution, diagnostics, renderAsset);
    if (diagnostics.length) { return { diagnostics }; }
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/naming-convention -- Pandoc's wire-format key.
    return { document: { 'pandoc-api-version': [1, 23, 1], meta: {}, blocks }, diagnostics };
}
```

The intended cases are deliberately narrow: nested prose and contributions,
ordinary code, stable document-to-document links, and exact semantic callback
inputs. Missing references, invalid modifiers, cycles, unsupported tokens,
Mermaid, and unresolved heading links block callbacks. HTML/TeX-looking ordinary
text remains data. Empty projects produce an empty Pandoc document. Font selection,
syntax highlighting, LaTeX assembly, process execution and export commands live
in their own adapters; images and tables remain unsupported.

## Heading contract tests

The heading tests stay close to the policy they exercise. Most run without Pandoc;
the optional real-tool case checks the JSON round trip together with LaTeX escaping
and destinations. A broader regression suite covers the complete document bridge.

The recursive test walker exposes blocks without changing their nesting in the
actual document. Production still traverses the complete Markdown token stream.

```ts : <<publication heading test dependencies>>=
import assert = require('node:assert/strict');
import { test } from 'node:test';
import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';
import { generateProject, parseProject, SourceDocument } from '../../engine';
import { preparePandoc, PandocBlock, PandocDocument } from '../../publication-pandoc';
import { renderLatex } from '../../publication-latex';
const source = (text: string, path = 'index.literate'): SourceDocument => ({ path, text });
const fence = (body: string) => '```ts : \<<piece>>=\n' + body + '\n```\n';
function blocks(document: PandocDocument): PandocBlock[] {
    const result: PandocBlock[] = [];
    function visit(block: PandocBlock): void {
        result.push(block);
        if (block.t === 'Div') { block.c[1].forEach(visit); }
        if (block.t === 'BlockQuote') { block.c.forEach(visit); }
        if (block.t === 'BulletList') { block.c.forEach(item => item.forEach(visit)); }
        if (block.t === 'OrderedList') { block.c[1].forEach(item => item.forEach(visit)); }
    }
    document.blocks.forEach(visit);
    return result;
}
```

Expected target encodings are derived from the public policy, not by importing a
private slug function. Every preparation also verifies snapshot immutability.

```ts : <<publication heading test helpers>>=
function target(slug: string, path = 'index.literate'): string {
    const hex = (value: string) => value.split('').map(c => c.charCodeAt(0).toString(16).padStart(4, '0')).join('');
    return 'h-' + hex(path) + '-' + hex(slug);
}
function prepare(sources: SourceDocument[]): PandocDocument {
    const project = parseProject(sources), before = JSON.stringify(project);
    const result = preparePandoc(project, () => [{ t: 'Para', c: [{ t: 'Str', c: 'CONTRIBUTION' }] }]);
    assert.deepEqual(result.diagnostics, []);
    assert.equal(JSON.stringify(project), before);
    assert.ok(result.document);
    return result.document;
}
function headingIds(document: PandocDocument): string[] {
    return blocks(document).flatMap(block => block.t === 'Header' ? [block.c[1][0]] : []);
}
function destinations(document: PandocDocument): string[] {
    return blocks(document).flatMap(block => block.t === 'Para' || block.t === 'Plain'
        ? block.c.flatMap(inline => inline.t === 'Link' ? [inline.c[2][0]] : []) : []);
}
```

Titles slug from parsed inline content. This covers entities, escapes, code,
link labels, nested formatting, Unicode normalization, symbols and empty titles.

```ts : <<publication heading slug tests>>=
for (const [title, slug] of [
    ['A *bold **inside*** and `x_y` [Label](https://example.org/ignored)', 'a-bold-inside-and-x_y-label'],
    ['Café 東京 Ελληνικά', 'café-東京-ελληνικά'], ['Cafe\u0301', 'café'],
    ['A &amp; B \\*literal\\*', 'a-b-literal'], ['~~Gone~~: A.B/C+D!', 'gone-abcd'],
    ['  A   B -- C_D  ', 'a-b----c_d'], ['😀 ♥ !!!', 'section'], ['', 'section'],
    ['__proto__', 'proto'], ['constructor', 'constructor'], ['İ', 'i\u0307'],
    ['`\\input{BAD}% & $ # ^ ~`', 'inputbad'], ['𝒜 𐐀', '𝒜-𐐨']
]) {
    test(`publication heading slug from ${JSON.stringify(title)}`, () => {
        const document = prepare([source('# ' + title + '\n\n[go](#' + encodeURIComponent(slug) + ')')]);
        assert.deepEqual(headingIds(document), [target(slug)]);
        assert.deepEqual(destinations(document), ['#' + target(slug)]);
        assert.match(headingIds(document)[0], /^h-[0-9a-f]+-[0-9a-f]+$/);
    });
}
```

Lowercasing can create a composable sequence (`J` plus caron), and removing
punctuation can bring a letter and combining mark together (`A.` plus acute).
Both composed and decomposed authored fragments must find the final NFC slug.

```ts : <<publication heading slug tests>>=+
for (const [title, slug] of [['J\u030c', '\u01f0'], ['A.\u0301', '\u00e1']]) {
    test(`final slug normalization resolves both fragment forms for ${title}`, () => {
        const links = [slug, slug.normalize('NFD')].map(value =>
            '[go](#' + encodeURIComponent(value) + ')').join(' ');
        const document = prepare([source('# ' + title + '\n\n' + links)]);
        assert.deepEqual(headingIds(document), [target(slug)]);
        assert.deepEqual(destinations(document), ['#' + target(slug), '#' + target(slug)]);
        assert.deepEqual(headingIds(prepare([source('# ' + slug)])), headingIds(document));
    });
}
```

Final normalization must also precede duplicate grouping, not merely lookup or
ID encoding. Canonically equivalent bases share numbered alternatives, and both
fragment spellings report the same ambiguity when no ordinal is supplied.

```ts : <<publication heading slug tests>>=+
for (const [title, slug] of [['J\u030c', '\u01f0'], ['A.\u0301', '\u00e1']]) {
    test(`final slug normalization groups canonical duplicates for ${title}`, () => {
        const headings = '# ' + title + '\n\n# ' + slug;
        const links = [slug, slug.normalize('NFD')].flatMap(value => [1, 2].map(ordinal =>
            '[go](#' + encodeURIComponent(value) + '-' + ordinal + ')')).join(' ');
        const document = prepare([source(headings + '\n\n' + links)]);
        const ids = [target(slug + '-1'), target(slug + '-2')];
        assert.deepEqual(headingIds(document), ids);
        assert.deepEqual(destinations(document), [...ids, ...ids].map(id => '#' + id));
        for (const value of [slug, slug.normalize('NFD')]) {
            const project = parseProject([source(headings + '\n\n[bad](#' + encodeURIComponent(value) + ')')]);
            const result = preparePandoc(project, () => { throw new Error('must not render'); });
            assert.equal(result.document, undefined);
            assert.deepEqual(result.diagnostics.map(d => [d.code, d.line]), [['ambiguous-publication-heading', 4]]);
            assert.ok(result.diagnostics[0].message.endsWith('use #' + slug + '-1, #' + slug + '-2'));
        }
    });
}
```

Markdown formatting remains in the Header AST, rather than being replaced with
the plain slug text. Setext and ATX spellings describe the same heading identity.

```ts : <<publication heading slug tests>>=+
test('heading formatting survives and setext identities match ATX', () => {
    const title = '*Strong* and `code` [label](https://example.org)';
    const atx = prepare([source('# ' + title)]);
    const setext = prepare([source(title + '\n====')]);
    assert.deepEqual(headingIds(atx), headingIds(setext));
    const header = blocks(atx).find(block => block.t === 'Header');
    assert.ok(header?.t === 'Header');
    assert.deepEqual(header.c[2].map(inline => inline.t), ['Emph', 'Space', 'Str', 'Space', 'Code', 'Space', 'Link']);
});
test('generated HTML has no heading IDs; publication does not change HTML behavior', () => {
    const result = generateProject([source('# Same\n\n# Same\n\n[go](#same)')]);
    assert.deepEqual(result.diagnostics, []);
    const html = result.files.find(file => file.path === 'index.html')!.content;
    assert.equal((html.match(/<h1>Same<\/h1>/g) ?? []).length, 2);
    assert.ok(html.includes('href="#same"'));
});
```

Natural suffixed names are reserved even when they occur after duplicates.
Normalization collisions and empty-title fallbacks use the same rule.

```ts : <<publication heading identity tests>>=
test('duplicates reserve natural suffixes and allocate deterministic unique IDs', () => {
    const text = '# Foo\n\n## Foo\n\n# Foo-1\n\n# Foo-2\n\n# !!!\n\n# 😀\n\n# section-1\n\n'
        + '[a](#foo-3) [b](#foo-4) [c](#foo-1) [d](#section-2) [e](#section-3)';
    const document = prepare([source(text)]);
    assert.deepEqual(headingIds(document), ['foo-3', 'foo-4', 'foo-1', 'foo-2', 'section-2', 'section-3', 'section-1'].map(s => target(s)));
    assert.equal(new Set(headingIds(document)).size, 7);
    assert.deepEqual(destinations(document), ['foo-3', 'foo-4', 'foo-1', 'section-2', 'section-3'].map(s => '#' + target(s)));
    const unicode = prepare([source('# Café\n\n# Cafe\u0301\n\n[a](#café-1) [b](#cafe%CC%81-2)')]);
    assert.deepEqual(headingIds(unicode), ['café-1', 'café-2'].map(s => target(s)));
    assert.deepEqual(destinations(unicode), headingIds(unicode).map(id => '#' + id));
});
```

Repeated naturally suffixed titles form their own duplicate group. Reserving
all bases also protects a literal second-level suffix from that group's ordinals.
Both ambiguous bases must name exactly the numbered destinations actually emitted.

```ts : <<publication heading identity tests>>=+
test('repeated Foo and Foo-1 reserve literal Foo-1-1 without target collisions', () => {
    const headings = '# Foo\n\n# Foo\n\n# Foo-1\n\n# Foo-1\n\n# Foo-1-1';
    const slugs = ['foo-2', 'foo-3', 'foo-1-2', 'foo-1-3', 'foo-1-1'];
    const links = slugs.map(slug => '[go](#' + slug + ')').join(' ');
    const document = prepare([source(headings + '\n\n' + links)]);
    assert.deepEqual(headingIds(document), slugs.map(slug => target(slug)));
    assert.equal(new Set(headingIds(document)).size, slugs.length);
    assert.deepEqual(destinations(document), headingIds(document).map(id => '#' + id));
    const project = parseProject([source(headings + '\n\n[bad](#foo) [bad](#foo-1)')]);
    const result = preparePandoc(project, () => { throw new Error('must not render'); });
    assert.equal(result.document, undefined);
    assert.deepEqual(result.diagnostics.map(d => [d.code, d.line, d.column]), [
        ['ambiguous-publication-heading', 10, 0], ['ambiguous-publication-heading', 10, 0]
    ]);
    assert.ok(result.diagnostics[0].message.endsWith('use #foo-2, #foo-3'));
    assert.ok(result.diagnostics[1].message.endsWith('use #foo-1-2, #foo-1-3'));
});
```

Document paths, not titles or chapter positions, scope identities. Forward links
and both existing local extension conventions resolve to the same destination.

```ts : <<publication heading identity tests>>=+
test('cross-document headings resolve before rendering with safe path aliases', () => {
    const document = prepare([
        source('[source](<chapters/a b.literate#same>) [html](chapters/a%20b.html#same)\n\n# Same'),
        source('[back](../index.html#same) [self](#same)\n\n# Same', 'chapters/a b.literate')
    ]);
    const home = target('same'), other = target('same', 'chapters/a b.literate');
    assert.deepEqual(headingIds(document), [home, other]);
    assert.deepEqual(destinations(document), ['#' + other, '#' + other, '#' + home, '#' + other]);
    const shifted = prepare([source('# Unrelated\n\n# Same', 'chapters/a b.literate'), source('\n\n# Same')]);
    assert.ok(headingIds(shifted).includes(home));
    assert.ok(headingIds(shifted).includes(other));
    const reordered = prepare([source('# Same', 'z.literate'), source('# Same', 'a.literate')]);
    assert.deepEqual(headingIds(reordered), [target('same', 'a.literate'), target('same', 'z.literate')]);
});
```

Path and slug punctuation never leaks into target syntax. Percent escapes are
URL syntax only and decoded once, not interpreted again as separators or code.

```ts : <<publication heading identity tests>>=+
test('hostile path characters and astral Unicode remain injective safe data', () => {
    const paths = ['a-b.literate', 'a/b.literate', 'é.literate', '𝒜.literate', 'a%20b.literate', 'a b.literate', 'x{y}&_.literate'];
    const links = paths.map(path => '[go](<' + encodeURI(path) + '#safe>)').join(' ');
    const document = prepare([source(links), ...paths.map(path => source('# Safe', path))]);
    assert.equal(new Set(headingIds(document)).size, paths.length);
    assert.deepEqual(destinations(document), paths.map(path => '#' + target('safe', path)));
    headingIds(document).forEach(id => assert.match(id, /^h-[0-9a-f]+-[0-9a-f]+$/));
});
```

A heading before and after a contribution must stay inside its original quote
and list item. Code containing fake headings contributes neither IDs nor aliases.

```ts : <<publication heading container tests>>=
test('nested headings and contribution slots retain the full container stream', () => {
    const text = '[before](#nested) [after](#after)\n\n> - ## Nested\n>\n'
        + '>   ```ts : \<<piece>>=\n>   # not a heading\n>   ```\n>\n>   ### After\n>\n>   tail\n';
    const document = prepare([source(text)]);
    assert.deepEqual(headingIds(document), [target('nested'), target('after')]);
    assert.deepEqual(destinations(document), headingIds(document).map(id => '#' + id));
    const quote = blocks(document).find(block => block.t === 'BlockQuote');
    assert.ok(quote?.t === 'BlockQuote');
    const list = quote.c[0];
    assert.ok(list.t === 'BulletList');
    assert.deepEqual(list.c[0].map(block => block.t), ['Header', 'Para', 'Header', 'Para']);
    assert.deepEqual(list.c[0][1], { t: 'Para', c: [{ t: 'Str', c: 'CONTRIBUTION' }] });
    assert.deepEqual(headingIds(prepare([source('```text\n# Fake\n```\n\n    # Also fake')])), []);
});
```

Failure assertions require no partial document and no contribution callbacks,
including when the invalid link occurs later than a valid contribution.

```ts : <<publication heading test helpers>>=+
function reject(text: string, code: string, line = 4): void {
    const project = parseProject([source(fence('ok') + '\n' + text)]);
    let calls = 0;
    const result = preparePandoc(project, () => { calls++; return []; });
    assert.equal(calls, 0);
    assert.equal(result.document, undefined);
    assert.ok(result.diagnostics.some(d => d.code === code && d.path === 'index.literate'
        && d.line === line && d.column === 0), JSON.stringify(result.diagnostics));
}
for (const href of ['#absent', 'index.html#absent', 'index.literate#absent', '#Title', '#caf%25C3%25A9']) {
    test(`missing heading diagnostic: ${href}`, () => {
        reject('[bad](' + href + ')\n\n# title\n\n# café', 'missing-publication-heading');
    });
}
```

Distinct diagnostics distinguish absent chapters/headings from unsafe or
unsupported URL syntax. Inject real tokens for values Markdown-it would otherwise
escape or refuse to parse as links.

```ts : <<publication heading diagnostic tests>>=
for (const href of ['#', 'index.html#', '#bad%00', '#bad%0a', '#bad%7f', '#bad%5c', '#%zz',
    '#a#b', '#a?b', '#a%20b', 'index.html?q=1#title', '../index.html#title',
    '/index.html#title', '//host/index.html#title', 'index.html%23title', 'index.html%3fq=1#title']) {
    test(`unsupported heading URL diagnostic: ${href}`, () => {
        const project = parseProject([source(fence('ok') + '\n[bad](https://example.org)')]);
        const inline = project.documents[0].tokens.find(token => token.type === 'inline')!;
        inline.children!.find(token => token.type === 'link_open')!.attrSet('href', href);
        let calls = 0;
        const result = preparePandoc(project, () => { calls++; return []; });
        assert.equal(calls, 0); assert.equal(result.document, undefined);
        assert.deepEqual(result.diagnostics.map(d => [d.code, d.line, d.column]), [['unsupported-publication-link', 4, 0]]);
    });
}
test('missing included document diagnostics cover whole and heading links', () => {
    reject('[bad](missing.html)', 'missing-publication-document');
    reject('[bad](missing.literate#title)', 'missing-publication-document');
});
```

The authoring path must reject unsafe local URL syntax too, not only injected
token attributes. These inputs survive Markdown-it as actual links and must
reach the publication gate without running an earlier contribution callback.

```ts : <<publication heading diagnostic tests>>=+
test('authored unsafe heading URLs are rejected before callbacks', () => {
    for (const href of ['#bad%00', '#bad%0a', '#bad%5c', 'index.html?q=1#title',
        '../index.html#title', '//host/index.html#title']) {
        reject('[bad](' + href + ')\n\n# Title', 'unsupported-publication-link');
    }
});
```

Ambiguous bases never silently select the first heading. Even a linked heading
with an unsupported child still goes through the ordinary inline validator.

```ts : <<publication heading diagnostic tests>>=+
test('ambiguous bases list allocated alternatives at the source link', () => {
    const project = parseProject([source('[bad](#foo)\n\n# Foo\n\n# Foo\n\n# Foo-1')]);
    const result = preparePandoc(project, () => []);
    assert.equal(result.document, undefined);
    assert.deepEqual(result.diagnostics.map(d => [d.code, d.line]), [['ambiguous-publication-heading', 0]]);
    assert.match(result.diagnostics[0].message, /use #foo-2, #foo-3/);
    reject('[bad](index.html#foo)\n\n# Foo\n\n# Foo', 'ambiguous-publication-heading');
});
test('heading indexing does not bypass unsupported inline content or raw attributes', () => {
    reject('# ![image](asset.png)', 'unsupported-pandoc-token');
    reject('# [bad](#absent)', 'missing-publication-heading');
    const project = parseProject([source('# Safe\n\n[go](#safe)')]);
    project.documents[0].tokens[0].attrSet('id', '\\input{ATTACK}');
    const result = preparePandoc(project, () => []);
    assert.deepEqual(result.diagnostics, []);
    assert.deepEqual(headingIds(result.document!), [target('safe')]);
});
```

The LaTeX adapter's existing preparation gate must prevent converter invocation
on every unresolved heading class. No special raw heading renderer is needed:
Pandoc owns heading and hyperlink escaping.

```ts : <<publication heading adapter tests>>=
test('LaTeX adapter gates missing, ambiguous and unsupported heading links before converter', async () => {
    for (const [text, code] of [
        ['[go](#absent)', 'missing-publication-heading'],
        ['[go](#same)\n\n# Same\n\n# Same', 'ambiguous-publication-heading'],
        ['[go](index.html?q=1#same)', 'unsupported-publication-link']
    ]) {
        let calls = 0;
        const result = await renderLatex(parseProject([source(fence('ok') + '\n' + text)]), {}, async () => {
            calls++; return 'must not happen';
        });
        assert.equal(calls, 0); assert.equal(result.latex, undefined);
        assert.ok(result.diagnostics.some(d => d.code === code));
    }
});
```

The real writer must preserve IDs, produce internal links to them, and escape
hostile heading and link-title text. JSON round-trip also catches malformed Pandoc
attributes that a mock converter could miss.

```ts : <<publication heading adapter tests>>=+
test('real Pandoc preserves heading destinations, links and escaped inline author data',
    { skip: process.env.LITERATE_TEST_PANDOC !== '1' }, () => {
        const document = prepare([
            source('[next](two.html#café)\n\n# `\\input{HEADING_ATTACK}` & 50% _value_\n\n[me](#inputheading_attack-50-value "\\\\input{TITLE_ATTACK}")'),
            source('# Café\n\n[back](index.literate#inputheading_attack-50-value)', 'two.literate')
        ]);
        for (const format of ['json', 'latex']) {
            const result = spawnSync('pandoc', ['--from=json', '--to=' + format, '--top-level-division=chapter'], {
                input: JSON.stringify(document), encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 15000, maxBuffer: 1024 * 1024
            });
            assert.ifError(result.error); assert.equal(result.status, 0, result.stderr);
            if (format === 'json') {
                assert.deepEqual(JSON.parse(result.stdout).blocks, document.blocks);
            } else { checkHeadingLatex(result.stdout, document); }
        }
    });
```

These assertions verify destinations and link commands, not merely that IDs
appear somewhere in output. A separate manual PDF proof can inspect annotations.

```ts : <<publication heading test helpers>>=+
function checkHeadingLatex(latex: string, document: PandocDocument): void {
    for (const id of headingIds(document)) {
        assert.ok(latex.includes('\\label{' + id + '}'), latex);
        assert.ok(latex.includes('\\hyperref[' + id + ']'), latex);
    }
    assert.ok(!latex.includes('\\input{HEADING_ATTACK}'));
    assert.ok(!latex.includes('\\input{TITLE_ATTACK}'));
    for (const escaped of ['\\textbackslash', '\\{', '\\}', '\\&', '50\\%']) {
        assert.ok(latex.includes(escaped), escaped + '\n' + latex);
    }
}
```
